window.docContents[118].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Green-Kernels-and-Poisson-Representation--Fundamental-Solution-Convolution":{"contents":"The representation-formula approach to existence starts here: given\n, the convolution  is the natural\nwhole-space candidate for . The proof has two steps. The\nderivative-transfer identity  uses only\nlocal integrability of  and the two continuous derivatives and\ncompact support of . The second step identifies  with\n through the distributional identity , which\nneeds singular-kernel analysis and holds in every positive dimension. Together\nthe two steps give classical inversion for every  and every\n (Evans, 2010, Theorem 1, pp. 23–25).\n\nFor a bundled test function , the classical\nwhole-space candidate is\n\nDifferentiating the convolution is legitimate because the derivative can be\nmoved onto the compactly supported smooth factor, whose translates stay in a\nfixed compact set as the field point varies locally; local integrability of\n then supplies the dominator. The transfer theorem records this for\niterated coordinate derivatives of arbitrary finite order.\n\nFor a locally integrable left factor and a right factor in\n, every finite iterated coordinate derivative\ntransfers to the right factor. For the datum  used\nin the Poisson construction, the separate one- and two-derivative transfer\nlemmas give the required identities without assuming infinite smoothness.\n\nCompact support confines every translated derivative of the right factor to\na fixed compact set locally in the field variable. Local integrability of\nthe left factor supplies an integrable dominator there. Differentiation under\nthe integral transfers one coordinate derivative to the right factor, and\ninduction repeats this for any finite derivative list.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Model Dirichlet Problems and Harmonic Rigidity\u0009Green Kernels and Poisson Representation","header":"5.3.1.4. Fundamental-Solution Convolution","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Green-Kernels-and-Poisson-Representation--Fundamental-Solution-Convolution"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Weak-Waves-by-Galerkin-Approximation--The-Galerkin-Construction-for-Weak-Waves":{"contents":"The construction follows the parabolic case with one difference:\nthe wave equation is second order in time, so both displacement and\nvelocity must be approximated simultaneously, and the energy identity\ninvolves kinetic and potential terms rather than a single dissipative form.\n\nAt dimension , one solves the system of  ODEs\n\n\n\nwith projected initial displacement and velocity. For an autonomous symmetric\nstiffness form , testing with  gives, almost everywhere,\n\n\n\nFor pivot-valued forcing in , Grönwall's inequality, coercivity of\n, and uniform projection stability give the bounds\n\nindependently of . Weak compactness in reflexive Bochner spaces extracts\na common limit.\n\nFix a finite interval  and forcing .\nA weak wave consists of displacement in , velocity in ,\nand acceleration in . Integrated time-derivative identities carry\nthe initial traces, and the spatial equation\n holds almost everywhere in .\n\nThe solution concept is stated for the limit object; the construction begins\none level down, with a finite-dimensional trial space  in which the\nabstract equation becomes a linear system of ordinary differential equations\nfor the coefficients.\n\nOn each finite-dimensional trial space, a bounded strongly measurable\nstiffness operator and an integrable dual forcing produce a global\nCarathéodory solution of the second-order Galerkin coefficient system on the\nchosen finite interval.\n\nThe second-order equation is rewritten as a first-order affine system for\ndisplacement and velocity. Finite-dimensional Carathéodory existence applies,\nand projection to the two state components recovers the two almost-everywhere\nequations and initial values.\n\nA coefficient trajectory lives in ; to compare it with the eventual\nlimit it must be read as an element of the three Bochner spaces in which weak\nwaves live.\n\nFor the acceleration realization, assume additionally that the ODE forcing is\nrepresented in  and that  is strongly measurable almost\neverywhere and essentially bounded. Under these hypotheses, a finite-dimensional\nGalerkin trajectory is realized canonically as a displacement in ,\na velocity in , and an acceleration in .\nThe acceleration is the residual projected through a stable trial projection,\nsince the finite system tests only against its own trial space. This\nprojected residual agrees almost everywhere with the coefficient\nacceleration embedded in .\n\nOnce realized in Bochner spaces, the Galerkin trajectory satisfies the same\ntwo integrated time identities that define a weak wave. The integrated form\nmatters: a boundary value of an  class has no meaning, whereas pairing\nagainst a time test  with  records the initial value\nunambiguously.\n\nWrite  for the inclusion, and let  be the\nfinite-level initial displacement and velocity. The Galerkin displacement\n, pivot velocity , and dual acceleration  satisfy, for\nevery  and time test  with ,\n\nPair the coefficient equations with an ambient test and integrate by parts\nagainst . The final-time term vanishes because ; the\ninitial terms are the pairings with  and . Almost-everywhere\nagreement of the canonical Bochner realizations gives the displayed identities.\n\nThe time identities hold against every , but the spatial equation\ndoes not: at finite level it is known only after projecting the test onto\n. What does survive uniformly is a bound, because the stable projection\nhas norm at most  independently of .\n\nUniform energy stability of the trial projection yields a\ndimension-independent  acceleration bound. The corresponding\nalmost-everywhere spatial equation evaluates the stiffness and forcing on\nthe projected ambient test.\n\nThe projected dual residual is the forcing minus the stiffness applied to the\nGalerkin displacement. Boundedness of the stiffness, stability of the trial\nprojection, and the displacement and forcing norms control this\nresidual in , with constants independent of dimension. Evaluating the\nprojection against a test gives the projected spatial equation.\n\nPassing to the limit is a closedness question in the weak topology. The\nintegrated identities and the equation are linear, so they survive weak\nconvergence once the operator field is essentially bounded.\n\nWith fixed forcing and common exact initial data, the weak-wave predicate is\nclosed under a common weak limit in\n, assuming the operator field is\nmeasurable and essentially bounded.\n\nBounded linear maps pass the displacement identity and the time-test velocity\nidentity to the limit. Bounded operator-valued multiplication passes\n weakly, and uniqueness of weak limits identifies the limiting\nacceleration equation.\n\nGalerkin approximations are not exact weak waves, since their spatial equation\nis only projected, so closedness alone does not apply to them. The missing\ningredient is strong convergence of the projections to the identity on :\npairing weakly convergent residuals with strongly convergent tests\n recovers the ambient equation in the limit.\n\nThe projected spatial equations pass to the full ambient equation when the\ndisplacements and accelerations have common weak limits, the displacement\nfamily is uniformly bounded, and the stable trial projections converge\nstrongly to the identity on . The coefficient field is measurable and\nessentially bounded.\n\nThe residuals  converge weakly in . Uniform projection\nstability lifts  to strong convergence on  tests.\nPairing the bounded weak residuals with those strongly convergent tests\nidentifies the acceleration limit with .\n\nCombining the uniform bounds with the two limit theorems yields existence.\n\nAssume a bounded projected Galerkin family with stable trial projections\nconverging to the identity. Every approximation must have the same exact\ninitial displacement  and velocity  in its integrated boundary\nidentities. Then it has a weak-wave limit with these data. Uniform\nalmost-everywhere energy bounds pass to the limit as\n and  bounds for displacement and velocity.\n\nWe follow Evans (2010, pp. 405–406) in the abstract Gelfand-triple setting.Uniform bounds. The assumed energy bounds give\n, and the accelerations are\nassumed bounded, , both independent\nof the trial dimension .Weak compactness. The spaces , , and\n are reflexive Banach spaces. The uniform bounds place the\nGalerkin family in a bounded ball of their product. Reflexive weak compactness,\napplied simultaneously to the three coordinates, gives weak limits\n, , .Passing the equation to the limit. Bounded linear time-test functionals\npass both integrated identities to the limit, retaining their fixed initial\nboundary terms. The projected spatial limit theorem\n supplies the ambient equation\nby weak–strong testing. Together these give the weak-wave predicate.Essential supremum bounds. The set of  functions satisfying\n almost everywhere is norm-closed and convex, hence\nweakly closed in . The displacement limit belongs to that same set.\nThe identical argument in  preserves the velocity bound.\n\nIn practice the Galerkin data are projections  and \nof the exact data, so they vary with . Strong convergence of the projected\ndata is enough to pass the initial boundary terms of the integrated identities\nto the limit.\n\nSuppose the projected Galerkin families have common weak limits, a uniform\ndisplacement bound, and stable trial projections converging to the identity.\nTheir initial data may vary with the approximation index, provided\n strongly in  and  strongly in . Then the\nlimits form a weak wave with initial data .The common weak limits are hypotheses; weak compactness extracts them from\nbounded families.\n\nPass the time integrals through weak convergence. Strong convergence of\n and  gives convergence of both initial boundary pairings against\neach fixed test. The projected spatial limit theorem\n supplies the remaining ambient\nequation under the same measurable, essentially bounded coefficient hypothesis.\n\nThe displacement of a weak wave is only an  class, so  has no\nmeaning at an individual time. The integrated displacement identity\nnevertheless says that  has a weak time derivative in , namely the\nembedded velocity, which is enough regularity to select a continuous\npivot-valued representative. The energy-uniqueness argument evaluates that\nrepresentative at a terminal time .\n\nEvery weak-wave displacement has a strongly continuous representative in the\npivot space on the closed time interval, agreeing almost everywhere with the\nembedded  representative and taking the prescribed initial value.\n\nApply the finite-interval continuous-representative theorem to the integrated\ndisplacement equation, with the pivot velocity embedded into  as its\nweak time derivative. This gives a continuous pivot-space representative\nagreeing almost everywhere with . The initial boundary term identifies\nits value at the lower endpoint with .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Weak Waves and Evolution\u0009Weak Waves by Galerkin Approximation","header":"7.3.1.2. The Galerkin Construction for Weak Waves","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Weak-Waves-by-Galerkin-Approximation--The-Galerkin-Construction-for-Weak-Waves"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Extension-and-Density-on-Regular-Domains":{"contents":"Whole-space approximation and embedding theorems become useful on a domain\n once a Sobolev function on  can be continued to all of\n without changing its almost-everywhere class on .\nThere are two distinct reasons to ask for such a continuation, and they require\ndifferent kinds of control.\n\nFor approximation of one fixed function, it is enough to know that some global\nSobolev representative exists. No estimate for the chosen extension is used:\none extends, applies the whole-space density theorem, and restricts the\napproximants. To transfer norm estimates, compactness, or nonlinear operations\nuniformly from  to , we use a bounded linear map\nbetween the completed graph spaces.\n\nWe therefore distinguish three mathematical conclusions. A\nrepresentative extension agrees\nwith the original function almost everywhere on the domain. A\nbounded extension operator makes this\nchoice linearly and controls the norm by one constant for every input. A\nsupported extension operator\nalso keeps the extensions inside a prescribed region, a property needed when\ncompactness arguments must prevent mass from escaping to infinity.\nBoundary charts and cutoffs construct the\nfirst-order operator with these controls.\n\nThe geometric hypothesis is operational. The extension construction assumes a\nfinite atlas\ncontaining globally represented chart maps, inverse maps, and the\npartition-of-unity cutoffs used by the proof. Every bounded open set with\n graph boundary admits such an atlas.\n\nThe local construction is easiest to see on an interval. For\n with , reflect across zero:\n\n\n\nThe two sides meet with the same value, so reflection creates no jump at\nzero. For finite , each integral in the first-order graph norm doubles:\n.\nIn particular, a constant reflects to a constant, whereas its zero extension\nhas a jump. A boundary chart performs this reflection after flattening the\nboundary, and cutoffs combine the reflected pieces.\n\n\n\nThe figure shows the two continuations side by side. The reflected\nderivative jumps at zero, which costs nothing in ; the jump of the zero\nextension puts a point mass into its derivative, so the zero extension of a\nfunction with nonzero boundary value is not in .\n\nThis first-order picture also explains an order restriction. Reflecting\n gives , whose second distributional derivative is\n. The same reflection therefore does not automatically extend\n. Higher-order extension needs additional matching of derivatives\nacross the boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings","header":"4.3.1. Extension and Density on Regular Domains","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Extension-and-Density-on-Regular-Domains"}});